%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 8de559a088a5d4d48bb00ae8ccc21165, type: 3}
  m_Name: Asteroid Strike
  crisisName: Asteroid Strike
  resolutionSplash: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 64a7a022259ddb04c80af00c2f0f3c1c, type: 3}
  crisisStartSplash: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e7fbab6ea9adf324d902ce281aca5a88, type: 3}
  obviousWarningSplash: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 1538cbdd3e8293f4caa45f3ef29ec3da, type: 3}
  ominousWarningSplash: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 18e74e0b42a2d9f408b97f6da31445cf, type: 3}
  ominousWarningText: "A new object has appeared in the heavens. At first it is faint enough that only careful observation reveals it, another point of light among thousands tracked by astronomers each night. Yet its speed and trajectory refuse to fit the patterns expected of ordinary celestial bodies, and new observations only make the calculations more troubling. Independent observatories repeat the measurements and quietly begin exchanging data, each hoping the others will discover an error. The projected path shifts slightly with every refinement, but always in the same directionâ€”closer to our world. Officials have not yet announced any danger to the public. Astronomers are beginning to suspect that this object may pass far too close."
  obviousWarningText: "The calculations are complete. Observatories across the world now reach the same conclusion using independent instruments and models: the object is on a collision course. The remaining uncertainty concerns where it will strike, how much energy it will release, and whether there is enough time to alter its path. Governments quietly move emergency planners into secure facilities while scientists debate interception methods that until now existed mostly in theory. The object has grown bright enough to be identified by experienced observers without specialized equipment, transforming an abstract prediction into something visible in the night sky. There is no longer serious disagreement about whether it is coming. The question is whether anything can still be done to stop it."
  crisisStartText: "There is no longer any doubt. A massive asteroid is on course to strike the planet, and current projections place the threatened continent directly beneath its path. A countdown has begun: if nothing changes before impact, cities, infrastructure, and populations across the target region may suffer catastrophic damage. Scientific and industrial capacity can be directed toward an interception attempt, while civilizations unable or unwilling to stop the asteroid can prepare shelters and harden vulnerable settlements. Every turn spent debating the response is a turn removed from the remaining window. For the first time in history, an enemy approaches that cannot be threatened, negotiated with, or frightened away."
  resolutionText: "The countdown has ended, one way or another. Wherever interception succeeded or the shelters held, cities that spent every remaining hour preparing are only now beginning to take stock of what survived. Debris still falls in scattered fragments across the affected regions, and the night sky carries a faint reminder of how close disaster once came. Engineers and scientists who worked without rest through the crisis are hailed as heroes, whatever the outcome, for having given our people a fighting chance against a threat no army could face directly. Families return to inspect homes, farms, and roads for damage, grateful simply to still have a place to return to. The stone that once threatened to end everything has passed, or been shattered, or been endured. The sky is empty again, and life resumes beneath it."
  ominousWarningTurns: 1
  obviousWarningTurns: 1
  durationTurns: 20
  useAgeWindow: 1
